  "The probe path (the planner scanning each clause to estimate it) is UNCHANGED code across all three runs, so its own movement is the host. It went 4043.2 -> 1104.1 -> 2051.7 ms, i.e. the host was 1.86x SLOWER during this run than during the per-pattern run, even though load average read lower. Dividing that out: execute improved 1.32x raw, so ~2.46x is attributable to the hash join. Cumulatively from the per-binding baseline, execute is 22.6x raw and ~11.5x once the host is divided out."
  :honest-summary "Hash join is worth ~2.5x on the two-hop join on top of batching's ~4.7x -- about 11.5x cumulative, not the 22.6x the raw numbers read as."
  :the-control-earned-its-keep-twice
  "Load average said this machine was quieter (30-35 vs 40-80) and the unchanged probe path said it was busier. The probe path was right: it is measuring the same work in the same process, and load average is not. Without it this receipt would claim 22.6x."}
 :one-hop-unchanged-by-design
 "The one-hop control moved 150.6 -> 196.5 ms raw, which is 0.98x once the host (1.28x slower by the same probe measure) is divided out -- no change, as intended. Its hasCreator step sees ~882 distinct keys against 7,355 rows, which is above the 4x budget, so it stays on the keyed path. The budget declining to fire is as much a result as it firing."
